# LINE ENDINGS ARE PART OF THE COMMIT, AND THE CHECKOUT MUST NOT REWRITE THEM.
#
# This rule was added on 2026-08-23 for EP-12, after measuring what the pipeline was actually
# verifying. Development happens on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, and this file previously pinned
# eol=lf for three globs only, so every other text file was converted on checkout. scripts/ci.mjs
# exports the commit under verification to a throwaway `git worktree` and builds the CI image from
# it — and that export is a checkout. At 5afcd00, 257 of 286 tracked files reached the container with
# different bytes than the commit they were said to be. The pipeline was verifying a transcription.
#
# It went unnoticed because almost nothing reads a source file as bytes. What found it was the
# mutation suite: two of its six checks search for a string anchored on a newline, and CRLF is not
# that string, so the inventory and fidelity gates had never once been attacked inside the container.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect binary content and leave it alone; there is none in this repository
# today, and the day there is, this line does the right thing without being edited. eol=lf makes
# every text file check out as it was committed, on every platform.
#
# This attribute is necessary and is not sufficient. It is a file like any other and can be edited
# away, so the property is also asserted at the boundary: scripts/ci.mjs compares every committed
# blob to the exported tree before the image is built and refuses the run on any difference. If you
# are reading this because that check went red, the answer is not to relax the check.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must check out with LF, on every platform.
#
# Development here happens on Windows, where git's autocrlf would hand `scripts/ci.sh` a CRLF line
# ending — including on the shebang line, where `#!/usr/bin/env sh\r` names an interpreter called
# "sh\r" and the script dies with a message that does not mention line endings. Nothing in this
# repository runs those scripts today, so the failure would wait until the first Linux checkout: a
# self-hosted runner, a colleague, or a container invoking ./scripts/ci.sh instead of node directly.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The Dockerfile and compose file are read by tooling that is happier with LF and never needs CRLF.
Dockerfile* text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
